  "id": "sentinel-2-l2a",
  "title": "Sentinel-2 L2A scenes (item index)",
  "description": "The AWS Earth Search item index for Sentinel-2 L2A, republished as one year-partitioned GeoParquet 2.0 table of 12,879,803 rows covering 2016 to 2019. One row per scene, carrying the whole STAC item: footprint, acquisition time, MGRS tile, cloud cover, the scene-classification percentages, and the complete upstream `assets` object as a JSON string. Every Cloud-Optimized GeoTIFF URL is therefore in the table -- no API call, no URL template to guess -- while the imagery itself stays in the `sentinel-cogs` bucket on AWS. Rows are ordered by month and then by a Hilbert index, so a reader prunes on both time and space. Years before 2019 are one items.parquet each. From 2019 each year is split by the UTM zone of s2:mgrs_tile (the leading one or two digits of the tile id): 2019\u20132020 into 4 files, z01-20.parquet (zones 1\u201320), z21-35.parquet (zones 21\u201335), z36-46.parquet (zones 36\u201346), z47-60.parquet (zones 47\u201360); from 2021 into 8 files, z01-15.parquet (zones 1\u201315), z16-20.parquet (zones 16\u201320), z21-31.parquet (zones 21\u201331), z32-35.parquet (zones 32\u201335), z36-40.parquet (zones 36\u201340), z41-46.parquet (zones 41\u201346), z47-52.parquet (zones 47\u201352), z53-60.parquet (zones 53\u201360). The current year adds live.parquet, the tail fetched daily since the last consolidation. There is no zone= directory: every part sits in year=YYYY/ and matches partition:glob, and a reader with a tile id opens only the part whose zone range holds it. Coverage before December 2018 is partial: nothing for 2015-2016 and part of 2017-2018, which is what Earth Search serves rather than a gap introduced here. Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data. Read the [agent guide](AGENTS.md) before querying: `sat:orbit_state` and `s2:granule_id` are NULL on newer items, and `assets` is a JSON string, not a struct.",
  "license": "CC-BY-SA-3.0-IGO",
  "keywords": [
    "sentinel-2",
    "l2a",
    "stac-geoparquet",
    "earth-search",
    "esa",
